          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kurt+G%C3%B6del"} Kurt Gödel
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/consistent+theory"} consistent theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            containing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peano+arithmetic"} arithmetic
            and whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            form a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/recursive+set"} recursive set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            that cannot be proven in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            itself, i.e., that no such theory “can demonstrate its own consistency” – can prove an arithmetic statement that encodes the assertion of its consistency.
          %p
            An incompleteness theorem can be read as an
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/undecidable+proposition"} undecidability result
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            in the theory such that neither
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            nor its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/negation"} negation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ¬
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg{p}
            have
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proofs"} proofs
            in the theory. As such, it is part of a long history; for example, axiom systems for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+geometry"} Euclidean geometry
            not including the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parallel+postulate"} parallel postulate
            might not decide the parallel postulate, as shown by models for both Euclidean and non-Euclidean geometry. What was novel about Gödel’s results is that they worked directly at the level of syntax and applied to any (effectively generated) extension of arithmetic, producing sentences which in effect imply their unprovability.
          %p
            To some extent, Gödel’s incompleteness theorems have always had an air of mystery about them, or at least a reputation of being exceedingly difficult or subtle. The major insight of Gödel, that items in formal logic can be encoded within the recursive structures afforded by arithmetic (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%C3%B6del+numbering"}> Gödel numbering
            ), is of course a signal achievement not to be underestimated, and the detailed working out of the result that the provability predicate can be encoded as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/recursive+set"} recursive set
            can be said to form the technical core of this work. But the punchline, in the form of his
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagonalization+argument"} diagonalization argument
            that produces a true
            %sup#fnref:1
              %a{:href => "#fn:1", :rel => "footnote"} 1
            but unprovable sentence, should be seen for what it is: one of many
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagonalization+arguments"} diagonalization arguments
            (including for example
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cantor%27s+theorem"} Cantor's theorem
          %p
            In an interview (
            %a{:href => "William+Lawvere#Interview07"}> Lawvere 07
            ) not long after Gödel’s 100th birthday,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"} William Lawvere
            answered the question
          %blockquote
            %p We have recently celebrated Kurt Gödel’s 100th birthday. What do you think about the extra-mathematical publicity around his incompleteness theorem?
          %p
            by saying (reproduced in
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#Lawvere69"} Lawvere 69 reprint, p. 2
          %blockquote
            %p
              “In ‘Diagonal arguments and Cartesian closed categories’ (
              %a{:href => "#Lawvere69"}> Lawvere 69
              ) we demystified the incompleteness theorem of Gödel and the truth-definition theory of Tarski by showing that both are consequences of some very simple algebra in the Cartesian-closed setting. It was always hard for many to comprehend how Cantor’s mathematical theorem could be re-christened as a ”paradox“ by Russell and how Gödel’s theorem could be so often declared to be the most significant result of the 20th century. There was always the suspicion among scientists that such extra-mathematical publicity movements concealed an agenda for re-establishing belief as a substitute for science. Now, one hundred years after Gödel’s birth, the organized attempts to harness his great mathematical work to such an agenda have become explicit.
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yuri+Manin"} Yuri Manin
            writes in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#Manin02"} Manin02, p. 7
          %blockquote
            %p Baffling discoveries such as Gödel’s incompleteness of arithmetic lose some of their mystery once one comes to understand their content as a statement that a certain algebraic structure simply is not finitely generated with respect to the allowed composition laws.
          %p
            This diagonalization result, so often obscured, should be laid bare as the simple piece of algebra that it is. We give one approach here, based on the algebra of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperdoctrines"} hyperdoctrines
          %h2#gdels_incompleteness_theorems Gödel's incompleteness theorems
          %h3#categorical_preliminaries Categorical preliminaries
          %p
            We define the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equational+theory"} equational theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi PRA
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{PRA}
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/primitive+recursive+arithmetic"}> primitive recursive arithmetic
            ) to be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial
            among
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theories"} Lawvere theories
            whose generator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            is a parametrized
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} natural numbers object
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-set"} hom-set
            of morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \to 1
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi PRA
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{PRA}
            is the set of equivalence classes of closed
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terms"} terms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/numerals"} numerals
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            will serve double duty in a moment, as being also the set of objects of the Lawvere theory.)
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+theory"} first-order theory
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peano+arithmetic"} Peano arithmetic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+hyperdoctrine"} Boolean hyperdoctrine
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi PRA
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi BooleanAlgebra
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T: \mathbf{PRA}^{op} \to BooleanAlgebra
          %p
            taking
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi j
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Ob
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi PRA
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j \in Ob(\mathbf{PRA}) = \mathbb{N}
            to the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(j)
            consisting of
            = succeed "-ary" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicates"} predicates
            in the language of PA, modulo provable equivalence in PA. If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi j
                  %mo →
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: j \to k
            is a morphism of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi PRA
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{PRA}
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \in T(k)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^\ast (R)
            denote
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(f)(R) \in T(j)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            along
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi PRA
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{PRA}
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi PRA
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi j
                  %mo ,
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi action
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{PRA}(j, k) \times T(k) \stackrel{action}{\to} T(j)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↦
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f, R) \mapsto f^\ast(R),
          %p
            which allows us to regard
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            as a
            = succeed "-module." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
