                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                                %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi PRA
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 0
                          %mo ,
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ×
                          %mi T
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mi action
                        %mtd
                          %mi T
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 0
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  Form(PA) \times \Phi(1) &amp; \stackrel{code \times 1}{\to} &amp; \mathbf{PRA}(0, 1) \times \Phi(1) &amp; \stackrel{num \times 1}{\to} &amp; Term(0) \times \Phi(1) &amp;  \stackrel{sub}{\to} &amp; \Phi(0) \\
                  \&amp; &amp; &amp; _\mathllap{1 \times [-]} \searrow &amp; \downarrow _\mathrlap{ev \times [-]} &amp; &amp; \downarrow _\mathrlap{[-]} \\
                  \&amp; &amp; &amp; &amp; \mathbf{PRA}(0, 1) \times T(1) &amp; \underset{action}{\to} &amp; T(0).
                  }
          %p
            In other words, for all formulas
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Form
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi PA
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \in Form(PA)
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Φ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \in \Phi(1)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi R
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi sub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi num
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi code
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \cdot R \coloneqq sub(num (code(F)), R)
          #eq:eqn1.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi F
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mi code
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [F \cdot R] = code(F)^\ast ([R])
          %p which will be used in our diagonalization result.
          %h3#diagonalization Diagonalization
          %p
            The following result is routine from general recursive-arithmetic properties of Gödel coding; it applies to any extension of PA (or just
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robinson+arithmetic"} Robinson arithmetic
          .num_lemma
            %h6#lemma Lemma
            %p
              There is a primitive recursive function (i.e., a morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d: 1 \to 1
              in
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi PRA
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{PRA}
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Φ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \in \Phi(1)
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi code
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi code
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \circ code(t) = code (t \cdot t)
              %sup#fnref:2
                %a{:href => "#fn:2", :rel => "footnote"} 2
          #fix.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              For every
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Φ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \in \Phi(1)
              there is a fixed point in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Φ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi(0)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Φ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s \in \Phi(0)
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %mo ⇔
                    %mi s
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s \Leftrightarrow s \cdot R
              (
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
              is provably equivalent to
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s \cdot R
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              Pick a formula
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Φ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \in \Phi(1)
              such that
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi d
                        %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [t] = d^\ast [R]
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo =
                      %mi t
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi t
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s = t \cdot t
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi s
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [s] = [s \cdot R]
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi t
                            %mo ⋅
                            %mi t
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                          %mtd
                            %mi code
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi t
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mo *
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi t
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mi equation
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ≔
                          %mtd
                            %mi code
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi t
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mo *
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msup
                              %mi d
                              %mo *
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi R
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi d
                            %mo ∘
                            %mi code
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi t
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mo *
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi R
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi functoriality
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi code
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi t
                            %mo ⋅
                            %mi t
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mo *
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi R
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi by
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mi the
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mi lemma
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi t
                            %mo ⋅
                            %mi t
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ⋅
                            %mi R
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mtd
                            %mi equation
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    [t \cdot t] &amp; = &amp; code(t)^\ast ([t]) &amp; equation\; (1) \\
                    \&amp; \coloneqq &amp; code(t)^\ast (d^\ast [R]) &amp; \\
                    \&amp; = &amp; (d \circ code(t))^\ast ([R]) &amp; (functoriality) \\
                    \&amp; = &amp; (code (t \cdot t))^\ast ([R]) &amp; (by\; the\; lemma)\\
                    \&amp; = &amp; [(t \cdot t) \cdot R] &amp; equation\; (1)
                    }
            %p as required.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              The proof of theorem
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#fix"}
              was written to lay bare its provenance as a special case of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere%27s+fixed+point+theorem"} Lawvere's fixed point theorem
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatory+algebra"} combinatory algebra
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#Yanofsky03"} Yanofsky 03
          %h3#incompleteness Incompleteness
          %p
            Recall there is also a Gödel coding of PA proofs (certain sequences of PA formulas),
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi code
                    %mo :
                    %mi Proof
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi PA
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi PRA
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} code: Proof(PA) \to \mathbf{PRA}(0, 1)
          .num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_2 Lemma
            %p
              There is a binary primitive recursive predicate
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Pf
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Pf
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Pf
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Pf(p, x)
              is true in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              is the code of a proof of a sentence (a closed formula) with code
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Prov
                      %mo ≔
                      %msub
                        %mo ∃
                        %mi p
                      %mi Pf
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi p
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Prov \coloneqq \exists_p Pf(p, x) \in T(1)
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                      %mo =
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi Prov
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R = \neg Prov \in T(1)
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              %strong (Gödel’s First Incompleteness Theorem)
              There exists a sentence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
              in the language of PA such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
              is logically equivalent to the PA-sentence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi code
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi s
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi Prov
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} code(s)^\ast (\neg Prov)
              (whose interpretation in the model
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
              is that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
              has no PA proof).
          %p
            Thus, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
            is false in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
            has a PA proof (so that PA proves a false statement in a model: is inconsistent). In classical logic, the only other alternative is that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
            is true in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              Indeed, let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              be a formula that represents the unary predicate
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi Prov
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg Prov
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
              be a fixed point in the sense
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi s
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [s] = [s \cdot R]
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#fix"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi s
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo =
                    %mi code
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi s
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi Prov
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [s \cdot R] = code(s)^\ast (\neg Prov)
              by equation
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:eqn1"} (1)
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L%C3%B6b%27s+theorem"} Löb's theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere%27s+fixed+point+theorem"} Lawvere's fixed point theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chaitin%27s+incompleteness+theorem"} Chaitin's incompleteness theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne+completeness+theorem"} Deligne completeness theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations+of+mathematics"} foundations of mathematics
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/platonism"} platonism
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic+universe"} arithmetic universe
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/introspective+theory"} introspective theory
          %h2#References References
          %ul
            %li#Lawvere69
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Diagonal Arguments and Cartesian Closed Categories
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://tac.mta.ca/tac/reprints/articles/15/tr15abs.html"} TAC
            %li#Manin02
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yuri+Manin"}> Yuri Manin
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Georg Cantor and his heritage
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0209244"} arXiv:math/0209244
            %li#Yanofsky03
              %p
                Noson S. Yanofsky,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A Universal Approach to Self-Referential Paradoxes, Incompleteness and Fixed Points
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0305282"} web
          %p
            A brief review discussion explicitly in the context of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              Erik Gregersen,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://www.britannica.com/EBchecked/topic/369221/foundations-of-mathematics/35468/Internal-language"} Internal lanuage
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+proof"} formal proof
            of the Gödel-Rosser incompleteness theorem in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Coq"} Coq
            is given in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Russell+O%27Connor"}> Russell O'Connor
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Essential Incompleteness of Arithmetic Verified by Coq
              = succeed "," do
                %em Theorem Proving in Higher Order Logics. TPHOLs 2005
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 3603
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://r6.ca/Goedel/goedel1.html"} web
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/cs/0505034"} arXiv:cs/0505034
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/11541868_16"} doi:10.1007/11541868_16
          %p Formal proof of the second incompleteness theorem:
          %ul
            %li
              Lawrence Paulson,
              %em A Mechanised Proof of Gödel’s Incompleteness Theorems using Nominal Isabelle
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://www.cl.cam.ac.uk/~lp15/papers/Isabelle/Goedel-ar.pdf"}> pdf
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            Unified account of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L%C3%B6b%27s+theorem"} Löb's theorem
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%C3%B6del%27s+second+incompleteness+theorem"} Gödel's second incompleteness theorem
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kripke+semantics"} Kripke semantics
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/provability+logic"} provability logic
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/guarded+recursion"} guarded recursion
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+algebraic+theories"} essentially algebraic theories
            which “self-internalize”::
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sridhar+Ramesh"}> Sridhar Ramesh
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Introspective Theories and Geminal Categories
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://escholarship.org/uc/item/3mn0c475"} escholarship:3mn0c475
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Ramesh-IntrospectiveTheories.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p
            The following contains a careful discussion of the incompleteness theorem in the context of categorical foundations using the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+topos"} free topos
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jim+Lambek"}> Jim Lambek
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Phil+Scott"} Phil Scott
              %em Reflections on Categorical Foundations of Mathematics
              , pp.171-185 in Sommaruga (ed.),
              = succeed "," do
                %em Foundational Theories of Classical and Constructive Mathematics
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.site.uottawa.ca/~phil/papers/LS11.final.pdf"} draft
          %p
            A
            %em bref survol technique
            of Gödel’s theorem within the landscape of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert%27s+program"} Hilbert's program
            can be found in the first chapters of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Yves+Girard"}> Jean-Yves Girard
              ,
              %em Le point aveugle I
              , Hermann Paris 2006. (
              %a{:href => "http://iml.univ-mrs.fr/~girard/cours/hermann.pdf.gz"}> chapters 1-2
              )
          %p
            See also the discussion in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/list-arithmetic+pretoposes"} list-arithmetic pretoposes
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maria+Maietti"}> Maria Maietti
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Joyal’s arithmetic universe as list-arithmetic pretopos
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/24/3/24-03abs.html"} TAC
            %li#vanDijkOldenziel20
              %p
                Joost van Dijk, Alexander Gietelink Oldenziel,
                %em Gödel’s Incompleteness after Joyal
                , arXiv:2004.10482 (2020). (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.10482"}> abstract
                )
          .footnotes
            %hr/
            %ol
              %li#fn:1
                %p
                  Assuming that the theory is consistent. If the theory is inconsistent, then it can prove falsity and thence anything, including any internal statements of consistency.
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:1", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
              %li#fn:2
                %p
                  This and the next lemma demonstrate the essential truth of Paul Taylor’s quip (Practical Foundations of Mathematics, p. 192): “Lemmas do the work in mathematics: Theorems, like management, just take the credit. A good lemma also survives a philosophical or technological revolution.”
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:2", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
